M | analysis-a-refusals-window-ends-where-its-probe-closed | src/analysis/syntactic/parser.cpp | tests/mutations/texts/analysis-a-refusals-window-ends-where-its-probe-closed.m1.before | tests/mutations/texts/analysis-a-refusals-window-ends-where-its-probe-closed.m1.after | RE-CUT P69 L2b2 (lane lm, 2026-11-16), main's ruling: THE ARM'S OWN MUTATION IS EQUIVALENT ALONE, PROVED BY READING parser.cpp (it SURVIVED in check-mutations run 20261116-213402-d2ea60b0, macos-arm64-debug, every case green). A refusal is handed out (`noteRefusalReach_`) by the operand-time ask or by the close. The ask notes the operand's position (`noteOperandPos(tokens.position())`) on the very conditions the early base ask marks `baseAsked` on, so wherever `operandPos()` is set the ask runs at once, and a refusal it hands out is handed out at that same position: `tokens.position()` IS `operandPos()` there. Where it approves, the close reruns the same four rules on the same type child and the same follower, and an operand can bind a name but never unbind one visible at its open, so the close hands out no refusal after it. A close that hands one out therefore saw no operand open, `operandPos()` is empty, and both spellings read `tokens.position()`. This M row turns the operand-time ask off (it returns after noting the position), so the refused probe reads its operand and the close hands the refusal out past the follower: there the window's end is what the claim reads, and only the arm's own text moves it.
