A | tests-harness-legs-reads-the-awake-clock-second | .harness-config/runner/actions/real-examples/c/sqlite/harness_legs.py | tests/mutations/texts/tests-harness-legs-reads-the-awake-clock-second.before | tests/mutations/texts/tests-harness-legs-reads-the-awake-clock-second.after | TEST-RED | dss_harness_test_sqlite_harness_legs | dss_harness_test_sqlite_harness_legs | 55 | tests/mutations/texts/tests-harness-legs-reads-the-awake-clock-second.diag | CLAIM AT tests/harness/test_sqlite_harness_legs.cpp, TEST TheResolverSelfTestPasses. The suspend a host recorded is read from two clocks, the one that counts a suspend and the one that does not, and the clock that does NOT count it is read FIRST: a deschedule between the two reads then only adds to the recorded suspend, which is judged against a floor. The arm reads the counting clock first, so a host that never slept, descheduled between the reads, records a negative suspend -- on Linux through CLOCK_BOOTTIME and CLOCK_MONOTONIC, on Darwin through CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW and CLOCK_UPTIME_RAW. The site is a Python program no compiler reads: the object that witnesses its mutation is the test's own, which the build declares as depending on the program's files (the top-level declaration `sqlite_harness_legs`), and the test runs the program of the tree it was configured from.
