A | tests-stamp-guard-reads-an-empty-entry-as-a-hand-over | cmake/DssBuildStampDeclaration.cmake | tests/mutations/texts/tests-stamp-guard-reads-an-empty-entry-as-a-hand-over.before | tests/mutations/texts/tests-stamp-guard-reads-an-empty-entry-as-a-hand-over.after | TEST-RED | dss_build_build_stamp_identity | dss_build_build_stamp_identity | 46 | tests/mutations/texts/tests-stamp-guard-reads-an-empty-entry-as-a-hand-over.diag | CLAIM AT tests/program/test_build_stamp_identity.cpp, TEST ConfigureAnEmptyHandOverEntryHandsNothingOver. An EMPTY `FETCHCONTENT_SOURCE_DIR_<NAME>` hands nothing over (the dependency is fetched); the arm reads it as a hand-over, whose directory would then be the source tree itself and excuse every directory in it. The arm reads the form the case pins -- an empty entry given on the COMMAND LINE (cache type UNINITIALIZED) -- because the whole defect (every empty entry a hand-over) stops the BUILD's own configure wherever a worker fetches its dependencies: FetchContent creates FETCHCONTENT_SOURCE_DIR_GOOGLETEST and _NLOHMANN_JSON empty, each is read as a hand-over of the source root, and the declaration check refuses 'the walk ... judged no directory at all -- the check collapsed' (MEASURED 2026-10-20, run 20261020-083820-4c6f6f70, Mac run 20261020-083825-f3a74c78; the arm passed on 2026-10-10 only because that worker was HANDED both dependencies). So a fetching configure of the build is that form's witness, and the arm's mutant leaves FetchContent's own empties (type PATH) alone. The site is a CMake script no compiler reads: the object that witnesses its mutation is the test's own, which the build declares as depending on the two stamp modules, and the test runs the self-test of the tree it was configured from.
